  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::06.5": "The thickness of the layer that can be used is much <strong>thinner</strong>.<br>This means the amount of light passing through the layer is <strong>greater</strong>.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::07.1": "Mixture of compounds → representation 3 (two ○●○ molecules and two ●○ molecules).<br>Mixture of a compound and an element → representation 5 (two ○●○ molecules and two ○○ molecules).",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::07.2": "Put the mixture of salt and sand into a beaker and add water. Place the beaker on a tripod and gauze and warm it gently with a Bunsen burner, stirring with a glass rod, so that all the salt dissolves. The sand does not dissolve.<br>Filter the mixture using a funnel and filter paper. The sand stays on the filter paper as the residue, and the salt solution passes through as the filtrate. Rinse the sand with a little clean water to remove any salt, then leave the sand on the filter paper to dry, e.g. in a warm place.<br>Pour the salt solution (filtrate) into an evaporating basin and heat it with the Bunsen burner on the tripod and gauze until about half the water has evaporated (the crystallisation point). Then leave the remaining solution to cool so salt crystals form, and pat the crystals dry with filter paper (or heat to dryness to obtain dry salt).",
