  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::08.4": "So that tellurium and iodine were placed in groups with elements that have similar properties – iodine has similar properties to the Group 7 elements (e.g. bromine), and tellurium to the Group 6 elements.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::08.5": "Argon has a full outer shell of electrons (eight outer electrons), which is a stable arrangement of electrons, so it does not easily gain, lose or share electrons.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::08.6": "Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::08.7": "∼115 (most of the atoms, 96%, have a mass number of 115).",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::09.1": "CaS",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::09.2": "The calcium atom loses two electrons from its outer shell and the sulfur atom gains these two electrons – two electrons are transferred from calcium to sulfur. Calcium forms a positive ion, Ca²⁺, and sulfur forms a negative sulfide ion, S²⁻, both with full outer shells.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p1f::09.3": "Graphite has a giant covalent structure. Each carbon atom forms three strong covalent bonds (shared pairs of electrons) with three other carbon atoms, forming layers of hexagonal rings. There are no covalent bonds between the layers, only weak forces, so the layers can slide over each other. Each carbon atom has one electron that is not used in bonding, which becomes delocalised, so there are delocalised electrons between the layers that can move through the structure.",
